  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Below normal temperatures with frost and freeze\nconcerns continue through Monday morning.\n\nSurface high pressure settles over the Mid-Atlantic today\nthrough Monday with below normal temperatures expected. High \ntemperatures today rise into the 50s to low 60s across the \nforecast area with overnight lows tonight dropping into the 30s \nto mid 40s. Additional Frost Advisories will likely be needed \nfor Monday morning where the growing season continues east of\nthe Blue Ridge. Some pockets could fall below freezing, but will\nneed to evaluate incoming guidance to determine if this will\noccur on a widespread enough basis to need a Freeze Warning.\n\nTemperatures will climb slightly Monday and Monday night, with \nadditional Frost Advisories appearing unlikely.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Potential for isolated showers and breezy \nconditions next week as a Nor'easter lurks offshore.\n\nParticularly for locations along or east of I-95, a developing \nNor'easter off of the Mid-Atlantic coast guides weather conditions \nlate next week. East of I-95, scattered shower potential, wind \nstrength, and coastal flood risk are dependent on the track of this \nsystem. Regardless of where this Nor-easter tracks, locations \nfurther inland, particularly west of the Blue Ridge and Catoctin \nmountain ranges, are expected to remain dry in part due to \ndownsloping west of these mountain ranges. Daily daytime \ntemperatures are expected to remain fairly consistent throughout \nthis time period reaching into mid to upper 60s (50s at higher \nelevations). Cooler than average overnight temperatures on Monday \npersist through the end of the week.\n\nAn omega block setup and a predominantly zonal flow next week \ngenerally leads to uncertain but weaker systems. Current guidance \ncontinues to exhibit moderate spread when it comes to the placement \nof the low and timing as it meanders along the coast. On Monday, a \ndeveloping low pressure system is located off the North Carolina \ncoast. During the middle of next week, this Nor'easter crawls \nnorthward, remaining off the Mid-Atlantic coast. Guidance differs \nmost at towards the end of the week and into the weekend. Most \nsolutions keep this Nor'easter well off shore, maintaining dry \nconditions throughout our region. Some guidance meanders the low \ntoward the coast Thursday and Friday, increasing the potential for \nisolated showers and breezier conditions exclusively along and east \nof the I-95 corridor. As for any marine and coastal flooding \nconcerns, see the corresponding discussions below.",
